  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... High pressure over the Great Lakes vicinity \ncontinues to gradually slide SE'wards this afternoon, while aloft \ntroughing remains overhead. This will keep enough dry air in place \nand subsidence aloft to preclude much in the way of a rain threat \nfor the next few days outside of a stray seabreeze shower or\ntwo. Have kept the forecast precip free through Sat given lack \nof confidence in any precip occuring. Otherwise light winds and \nseasonable temps are forecast into the weekend. Highs today have\ngotten into the mid 80s and expect highs through Sat to \ngenerally remain between the mid 80s to low 90s while lows \nremain in the mid 60s to low 70s. Will note, with calm winds \nand mo clear skies tonight and low level moisture from the \nrecent rains sticking around, can't rule out some patchy shallow\nground fog tonight, though not expecting much in the way of \nimpacts from this regardless of development. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... We get into a more unsettled pattern starting on \nSunday as an upper level trough dives SE'wards from the Upper \nMidwest and tracking into the Tennessee River Valley this weekend \nwith multiple shortwaves then rotating around the trough axis from \nSunday into midweek next week. At the surface, a weak warm front \nwill lift north through the area Sat, while associated weakening sfc \nlow approaches the Mid-Atlantic. Guidance has begun keying in on a \nweak coastal trough lifting north across the region on Sun bringing \nthe first initial round of showers and thunderstorms to the area with \nadditional rounds of showers and thunderstorms possible into mid week \nnext week as the aforementioned boundary likely stalls west of the \narea and eventually dissipates. Will note given the scattered \nnature of the activity showers and storms will be hit or miss.",
